From: HungYuKao
 <jk@2001-b011-200a-b548-1959-a064-5756-bbb4.dynamic-ip6.hinet.net>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 20:52:36 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] audio: the stream timer wakes its client on a clock

The timer that signals an event-driven client each device period slept
usleep(10000) between wakes, about 11 ms on the phone: 900 to 920 wakes
in ten seconds instead of 1000. Wine's spatial audio stream renders one
period per wake, so DRAGON QUEST VII Reimagined Demo's 12-channel stream
got 88 to 92 % of real time and its sound broke up from the opening
movie on. The timer now waits until each period's deadline on the Mach
clock; a wake more than four periods late starts the clock again.

Class: fix
Evidence: docs/decisions/0199-spatial-audio-starts-each-update-silent.md
Offered-upstream: no
---
 build/ntdll-unix/audio_null_ios.c | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/build/ntdll-unix/audio_null_ios.c b/build/ntdll-unix/audio_null_ios.c
index 6d402e4..7d25d7a 100644
--- a/build/ntdll-unix/audio_null_ios.c
+++ b/build/ntdll-unix/audio_null_ios.c
@@ -1488,10 +1488,21 @@ static void ios_timer_loop(void *arg) {
     /* A Wine system thread started at the stream's first start. Wake an
      * event-driven client every device period so it refills the ring; exit
      * when release_stream marks the stream dead (it then joins us). */
+    /* The wakes keep to a clock, not to sleeps: a client that renders one
+     * period per wake (Wine's spatial audio stream does) gets exactly the
+     * device rate. usleep(10000) slept about 11 ms on the phone, and such a
+     * client delivered 91 % of real time. A wake more than a few periods late
+     * (the thread was held, the app was in the background) starts the clock
+     * again rather than firing the missed ones in a row. */
     struct ios_stream *s = arg;
+    uint64_t period = ios_ns_to_mach(IOS_RT_PERIOD_NS), next = mach_absolute_time();
     LOG_FN_CALL(9, "timer_loop");
     while (s->valid) {
-        usleep(10000); /* device period, 10 ms */
+        uint64_t now;
+        next += period;
+        mach_wait_until(next);
+        now = mach_absolute_time();
+        if (now > next + 4 * period) next = now;
         if (s->event && s->started) {
             NtSetEvent(s->event, NULL);
             atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&s->flow_events, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
